<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Free Officers Movement (Egypt)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1949–1952</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-officers-movement-iraq">Free Officers Movement</a> was a secret group of young Egyptian army officers, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> with General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-naguib">Muhammad Naguib</a> as its figurehead. Embittered by defeat in the 1948 war and by royal corruption, they seized power on 23 July 1952, forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farouk-of-egypt">King Farouk</a> to abdicate and in 1953 abolished the monarchy, ruling through a Revolutionary Command Council.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/free-officers-movement-egypt.jpg" alt="Free Officers Movement (Egypt)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Egypt_(1952%E2%80%931958).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
